SUMMARY OF NEW PARSER CHANGES

The following changes affect the intermediate, and will need to be addressed:

1. Creating a set from a long integer type is fixed.

2. The iproc and ifunc intermediates now have a "static" flag at the end.
This flag indicates that the procedure or function can be allocated statically,
but otherwise can be ignored.

[read into added fields in type, otherwise unused]

3. iappend is added. It expects a file on stack top. The file is moved to the
end and opened for writing.

[stubbed]

4. isemaphore is added. This is a type, and occupies a single byte.

[stubbed]

5. isignal is added. It expects a semaphore address on stack, and generates
a signal() call.

[stubbed]

6. isignalone is added. It expects a semaphore address on stack, and generates
a signalone() call.

[stubbed]

7. iwait is added. It expects a semaphore address on stack, and generates
a wait() call.

[stubbed]

8. itrybgn is added. It opens a try..except sequnce. It is terminated by itryend.
In between, it may have itryexp (except), and ielse (else). It behaves like
a simple block, with either the else clause or following to the next statement.

[stubbed]

9. ithrow is added. It generates a call to ithrow, which (since it is not system
dependent), is found in maclib.

[stubbed]

10. iassert is added. It expects a boolean on stack. It generates a call
to assert(), which is likely in syslib.

[stubbed]

11. icassrng is added. This is an entry instead of icassint, and has both a start
and end value, instead of just a case value. It is to be treated the same as
if a range of values between the start and end values were declared as cases.

[stubbed]

12. The iproc and ifunc intermediates now have a "virtual" flag at the end
(after the new "static" flag above), then a link to the type entry for either
the virtual or the override routine. Each virtual and override routine in a
form a pair that link to each other. There is no "override" flag, but having a
override link but no virtual flag (or external) means it is the override half of
the pair. When the block containing an override procedure/function is
initalized, it must hook the overrided routine, probally via a call to
_override_<x>. When a block contains a virtual call, it must provide a vectored
version of the routine, an override call, and an inherited call. Two new 
intermediates exist for inherited calls, iprccalo and ifnccalo (original). These
tolkens call the original version of the routine. There is no indirect call,
because procedure and function parameters always pass the vectored call.

[read into added fields in type, otherwise unused]

13. iclass, iatom, and ithread types are added, for each of the class marker 
types. Each of ivar, iproc and ifunc have a new link at the bottom that carries
the address of the marker types. This is required because each of these types
are treated differently when they are part of class. Variables are addressed as
record offsets from the object. Procedures and functions have special calling
and handling conventions.

[stubbed]

14. The iobjoff operator is added. The format is:

iobjmem <addr> <addr>

Expects the referenced address of an object on the stack in tagged format. 
The tag, in this case, contains the indication of what the actual object
pointed to is. The first address parameter is the class of the object. The
second address parameter is the variable within the class.

[stubbed]

15. The iis operator is added. The format is:

iis <addr>

Expects the referenced address of an object on the stack in tagged format.
The address parameter is the class for which the reference is to be checked.
If the reference is the same as, or contains, the given class, then the result
is true, otherwise false. The reference pointer is removed from the stack, and
a boolean result replaces it.

[stubbed]

16. The iprcbgn and ifncbgn intermediates no longer have the procedure or function
type embedded in the intermediate. The reason is that there is no way to know 
what procedure or function is going to be called during the parameter list build
in the case of overloads.